        <div class="scls-hero-text">
            <div class="eyebrow">Scientific Software Stack</div>
            <h1>Scientific Core Library Stack {{ release_version }}</h1>
            <p class="lead">SCLS packages a coherent stack of scientific libraries &mdash; BLAS/LAPACK, MPI, sparse solvers, PETSc, HDF5, and their dependencies. It provides RPMs for Enterprise Linux, DEBs for Ubuntu 24.04 LTS, and source-built installs for macOS and other Unix systems. After enabling a public binary repository, one package-manager command installs a complete, mutually compatible flavor.</p>

            <p>Public Linux binary flavors install under <code>/opt/scls/&lt;flavor&gt;</code>, so several stacks can coexist on the same host without interfering with the operating system. Source-built site flavors may choose their own prefix, and the macOS flavor installs directly under <code>/opt/scls</code>.</p>

            <p>Send comments, questions, or suggestions to Christian Messe: <b id="email">[javascript protected email]</b><noscript> cmesse (at) lbl.gov</noscript></p>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="section-band">
    <div class="section-head">
        <h2>Purpose</h2>
        <span class="caption">What SCLS solves</span>
    </div>
    <p>SCLS exists for the practical problem of getting scientific libraries to build, install, and work together on real machines. It targets environments where the system packages are too old, incomplete, inconsistent across dependencies, or simply unavailable for the platform in front of you.</p>
    <p>SCLS is intended for scientists, research software engineers, and HPC maintainers who need PETSc, HDF5, NetCDF, OpenMPI, sparse solvers, and math libraries to agree on compiler and ABI choices, MPI, BLAS/LAPACK, rpaths, and install layout.</p>
    <p>The stack covers the core numerical infrastructure used by downstream scientific software: BLAS/LAPACK, MPI-enabled math libraries, sparse solvers, graph partitioners, I/O libraries, and the dependencies needed to make them coexist. SCLS is not three separate build systems: RPM packages, DEB packages, and direct Unix installs are generated from the same package recipes and flavor definitions, which keeps Linux and macOS behavior aligned.</p>
    <p>SCLS ships as OS-level packages where possible rather than as Docker images or Spack environments. Docker is useful when an application brings its whole runtime with it, but containers can complicate MPI launches and shift stack management from the OS package manager to a container runtime. Spack is well suited to per-user, bespoke concretizations of a large software graph; SCLS instead favors a stable shared stack. RPMs and DEBs fit existing sysadmin workflows, provide normal install and uninstall semantics, and let users and projects share one managed binary installation in a predictable prefix.</p>
    <p>On macOS, SCLS does not use Homebrew as the stack manager for the same reason. Homebrew puts user tools and scientific libraries in one shared prefix (<code>/usr/local</code> on Intel Macs and <code>/opt/homebrew</code> on Apple Silicon), which makes a controlled, version-pinned scientific stack awkward. Keeping the macOS stack in the SCLS-owned <code>/opt/scls</code> prefix gives it the same isolation and activation model as the Linux binaries, so downstream code and environment variables behave consistently across platforms.</p>
    <p>The intended result is not maximum configurability. It is a coherent, reproducible stack that downstream codes can link against without constant manual repair.</p>

</section>

<section class="section-band">
    <div class="section-head section-head--accent">
        <h2>Design Philosophy</h2>
        <span class="caption">Curated, opinionated defaults</span>
    </div>
    <p>SCLS is deliberately opinionated. It is a curated build and packaging system, not a general-purpose meta-build framework. It makes a small number of project-wide choices so the resulting libraries share the same compiler assumptions, math backend, integer model, MPI implementation, and installation layout:</p>
    <ul>
        <li>GCC is the default toolchain because it is widely available, reliable, and has strong Fortran support.</li>
        <li>The math backend is selected by flavor (OpenBLAS for <code>gcc</code>, Intel MKL for <code>mkl</code>, reference BLAS/LAPACK for <code>debug</code>), not toggled package by package.</li>
        <li>OpenMPI is the default MPI implementation.</li>
        <li>Shared libraries are the normal target.</li>
        <li>LP64 (32-bit BLAS/LAPACK integer indices) is the default; ILP64 can be turned on optionally in the build scripts.</li>
        <li>Numerical correctness matters more than aggressive flag games &mdash; no <code>-ffast-math</code>.</li>
        <li>Binaries are built from explicit recipe dependencies, not from whatever optional libraries the build host happens to expose through autodetection.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The stack also deliberately leaves some things out:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>No Python in the stack.</strong> Interpreter choice (system, pyenv, conda, Spack, modules) is site- and user-specific, so recipes disable Python and other language bindings by default. Users bring their own interpreter and link it to the installed C/C++ libraries.</li>
        <li><strong>No Boost.</strong> Modern C++ (C++17/20) covers most of what scientific code once used Boost for, and the libraries SCLS targets either never required it or have dropped the dependency.</li>
        <li><strong>No GPL-3 linkable libraries in binary flavors</strong>, so downstream codes can link SCLS without copyleft concerns. GPL-3 build tools are fine because they run during the build and are not linked into the delivered libraries; GPL-2, LGPL, and CeCILL-C scientific libraries are allowed when their source and redistribution obligations are practical to satisfy. See the <a href="https://github.com/cmesse/scls/blob/main/doc/LICENSE_POLICY.md">license policy</a> for details.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>This bias toward curation is intentional. If a project needs many custom feature toggles for a single package, SCLS is probably the wrong tool. If it needs PETSc, HDF5, OpenBLAS, NetCDF, MUMPS, and related libraries to build and coexist predictably, SCLS is built for that job.</p>

</section>

<section class="section-band">
    <div class="section-head">
        <h2>Flavors</h2>
        <span class="caption">Compiler, math backend, and prefix</span>
    </div>
    <p>A <em>flavor</em> is the unit of consistency in SCLS. It defines the target platform, compiler family, optimization flags, math backend, MPI choice, integer model, and install prefix for the entire stack. Public Linux binary flavors install into distinct <code>/opt/scls/&lt;flavor&gt;</code> prefixes; source-built flavors declare their own prefix.</p>
    <ul>
        {% for flavor in flavor_groups %}
        <li><code><b>{{ flavor.package }}</b></code> &mdash; {{ flavor.label }}. {{ flavor.description }}</li>
        {% endfor %}
    </ul>
    <p>The public binary repositories currently target Enterprise Linux 9 and 10, Amazon Linux 2023, and Ubuntu 24.04 LTS. The SCLS source tree can also build local RPMs on other RHEL-family systems, local DEBs on Debian-family systems, and direct Unix installs on macOS and other Unix systems.</p>
    <p>Additional source-build flavors include <code>lbl</code> for an LBL-specific prefix, <code>intel</code> for Intel compiler builds, <code>gcc-mkl-cuda</code> for CUDA-enabled experiments, and <code>macos</code>. See the <a href="https://github.com/cmesse/scls/tree/main/flavors"><code>flavors/</code></a> directory for the complete list.</p>
    <p>CUDA binaries are not distributed, because a CUDA-linked stack would tie users to a specific driver and toolkit version. Users who need GPU acceleration can use the <code>gcc-mkl-cuda</code> source-build flavor and link against a locally installed CUDA toolkit.</p>
    <p>On macOS, the stack uses OpenBLAS rather than Apple's Accelerate framework. The <code>macos</code> flavor builds its own GCC for Fortran support. Pairing GCC-built Fortran with Accelerate is fragile: Accelerate is tuned for Apple's Clang toolchain, and its ABI is awkward to use from GCC. OpenBLAS works reliably with GCC, keeps math behavior aligned with the Linux binary flavors, and favors compute throughput over the performance and power tradeoffs that shape Accelerate.</p>
    <p>The Unix build script also includes GPL-3 build tools and selected LGPL-3 arbitrary-precision dependencies that are excluded from the public Linux binary flavors. See the source-only package table below.</p>

</section>

<section class="section-band">
    <div class="section-head section-head--accent">
        <h2>Installation on Enterprise Linux 9 / 10 and Amazon Linux 2023</h2>
        <span class="caption">RPM binary repositories</span>
    </div>
    <p>First, install the release package for your distribution (choose one):</p>
    <p>Enterprise Linux 9:</p>
    <pre class="command"><code>sudo dnf install <a href="https://belfem.lbl.gov/scls/el9/x86_64/Packages/scls-release-{{ scls_release_pkg_version }}-1.el9.noarch.rpm">https://belfem.lbl.gov/scls/el9/x86_64/Packages/scls-release-{{ scls_release_pkg_version }}-1.el9.noarch.rpm</a></code></pre>
    <p>Enterprise Linux 10:</p>
    <pre class="command"><code>sudo dnf install <a href="https://belfem.lbl.gov/scls/el10/x86_64/Packages/scls-release-{{ scls_release_pkg_version }}-1.el10.noarch.rpm">https://belfem.lbl.gov/scls/el10/x86_64/Packages/scls-release-{{ scls_release_pkg_version }}-1.el10.noarch.rpm</a></code></pre>
    <p>Amazon Linux 2023:</p>
    <pre class="command"><code>sudo dnf install <a href="https://belfem.lbl.gov/scls/amzn2023/x86_64/Packages/scls-release-{{ scls_release_pkg_version }}-1.amzn2023.noarch.rpm">https://belfem.lbl.gov/scls/amzn2023/x86_64/Packages/scls-release-{{ scls_release_pkg_version }}-1.amzn2023.noarch.rpm</a></code></pre>
    <p>Then install a flavor:</p>
    <pre class="command"><code>sudo dnf install scls-&lt;flavor&gt;</code></pre>
    <p>RPM builds also provide an optional examples meta-package. Install <code>scls-&lt;flavor&gt;-examples</code> if you want the upstream PETSc, SLEPc, and SUNDIALS example sources on disk.</p>
    <p>After installation, activate the environment:</p>
    <pre class="command"><code>source /opt/scls/&lt;flavor&gt;/share/scls/activate</code></pre>
    <p>This adds the stack's <code>bin/</code> directory to <code>PATH</code>, exposes package metadata through <code>PKG_CONFIG_PATH</code> and <code>CMAKE_PREFIX_PATH</code>, and sets <code>SCLS</code> and <code>SCLS_FLAVOR</code>. Runtime library lookup uses rpaths, not <code>LD_LIBRARY_PATH</code>. Once the environment is active, run <code>scls help</code> to list the runtime subcommands: <code>scls flavor</code>, <code>scls list</code>, <code>scls info &lt;package&gt;</code>, <code>scls env</code>, <code>scls prefix</code>, <code>scls license</code>, and <code>scls deactivate</code>.</p>

</section>

<section class="section-band">
    <div class="section-head">
        <h2>Installation on Ubuntu 24.04 LTS</h2>
        <span class="caption">DEB binary repository</span>
    </div>
    <p>Ubuntu 24.04 LTS (Noble Numbat) is the supported Debian-family target. Unlike <code>dnf</code>, <code>apt</code> does not install packages directly from a URL. Download and install the keyring and repository-configuration package first:</p>
    <pre class="command"><code>wget <a href="https://belfem.lbl.gov/scls/ubuntu/pool/main/s/scls-archive-keyring/scls-archive-keyring_{{ scls_release_pkg_version }}-1_all.deb">https://belfem.lbl.gov/scls/ubuntu/pool/main/s/scls-archive-keyring/scls-archive-keyring_{{ scls_release_pkg_version }}-1_all.deb</a>
sudo apt install ./scls-archive-keyring_{{ scls_release_pkg_version }}-1_all.deb</code></pre>
    <p>This installs the SCLS public key at <code>/etc/apt/keyrings/scls-archive-keyring.gpg</code> and a deb822-format <code>scls.sources</code> file at <code>/etc/apt/sources.list.d/</code>. Then refresh apt and install a flavor:</p>
    <pre class="command"><code>sudo apt update
sudo apt install scls-&lt;flavor&gt;</code></pre>
    <p>DEB builds produce per-package example subpackages when recipes define them, such as <code>scls-&lt;flavor&gt;-petsc-examples</code>. Unlike the RPM distribution, they do not currently provide an all-examples meta-package.</p>
    <p>After installation, activate the environment the same way as on Enterprise Linux:</p>
    <pre class="command"><code>source /opt/scls/&lt;flavor&gt;/share/scls/activate</code></pre>
    <p>The runtime <code>scls</code> command, the per-flavor prefix layout, and the activation script are identical between the RPM and DEB binary distributions.</p>

</section>

<section class="section-band">
    <div class="section-head section-head--accent">
        <h2>Building from Source and Local Packages</h2>
        <span class="caption">macOS and other Unix systems</span>
    </div>
    <p>Outside the public Enterprise Linux and Ubuntu repositories, clone the SCLS source tree and use the <code>./scls</code> wrapper. It selects RPM mode on supported RHEL-family hosts, including Amazon Linux 2023; DEB mode on Debian-family systems; and direct Unix-install mode elsewhere.</p>
    <p>On Debian-family systems outside the public Ubuntu 24.04 repository, <code>./scls</code> can still build local <code>.deb</code> packages. On macOS and other Unix systems, the Unix builder installs the packages directly into the selected SCLS prefix.</p>
    <p><strong>macOS support is currently beta</strong> and has been developed and tested on Intel Macs. Apple Silicon is not yet verified: the <a href="https://github.com/Homebrew/homebrew-core/tree/main/Patches/gcc">Apple Silicon GCC patch maintained by Homebrew</a> is vendored and applied automatically on arm64 hosts, but no Apple Silicon build has been run.</p>
    <p>The macOS flavor assumes familiarity with a Unix toolchain: installing Xcode Command Line Tools, understanding linker and sysroot settings, and interpreting build logs that point to an SDK or path issue. It builds its own GCC for Fortran support, which adds time to the first build. If that bootstrap fails on an Intel Mac (Apple Clang building GCC 16), Homebrew's prebuilt GCC is supported as a fallback. See the <a href="https://github.com/cmesse/scls/blob/main/doc/MACOS_BUILD.md">macOS build guide</a> for prerequisites, the step-by-step flow, and the two Homebrew fallback routes.</p>
    <p>Quick start:</p>
    <ol>
        <li>Clone the repository:<br>
            <pre class="command"><code>git clone https://github.com/cmesse/scls.git
cd scls</code></pre></li>
        <li>Create <code>flavor.conf</code> for your platform. Linux default / local package build:<br>
            <pre class="command"><code>echo "flavor: gcc" &gt; flavor.conf</code></pre>
            macOS:
            <pre class="command"><code>echo "flavor: macos" &gt; flavor.conf</code></pre></li>
        <li>Build and install the full flavor in dependency order:<br>
            <pre class="command"><code>./scls build all</code></pre>
            For a granular or resumable flow, build and install the next package:
            <pre class="command"><code>./scls build next
./scls install next</code></pre>
            Or build a specific package:
            <pre class="command"><code>./scls build petsc
./scls install petsc</code></pre></li>
        <li>Activate the environment. Linux / local package flavors:<br>
            <pre class="command"><code>source /opt/scls/&lt;flavor&gt;/share/scls/activate</code></pre>
            macOS:
            <pre class="command"><code>source /opt/scls/share/scls/activate</code></pre>
            Use <code>&lt;prefix&gt;/share/scls/activate</code> for any source-built flavor with a custom prefix.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>The wrapper also supports <code>./scls build</code>, <code>./scls install</code>, <code>./scls spec</code> (RPM mode), <code>./scls list</code>, <code>./scls order</code>, and <code>./scls check-updates</code>. Run <code>./scls</code> with no arguments to see the usage summary.</p>

</section>

<section class="section-band">
    <div class="section-head">
        <h2>Using the Stack</h2>
        <span class="caption">Activation and build integration</span>
    </div>
    <p>Once the environment is active, the stack is visible to ordinary build tools. A minimal PETSc example:</p>
    <pre class="command"><code>source /opt/scls/gcc/share/scls/activate
mpicc myapp.c -o myapp $(pkg-config --cflags --libs petsc)
mpirun -n 4 ./myapp</code></pre>
    <p>CMake projects can use the activated prefix directly:</p>
    <pre class="command"><code>cmake -S . -B build -DCMAKE_PREFIX_PATH="$SCLS"
cmake --build build</code></pre>
   
    <p>The activation script sets <code>PATH</code>, <code>PKG_CONFIG_PATH</code>, and <code>CMAKE_PREFIX_PATH</code>. It exports <code>SCLS</code> and <code>SCLS_FLAVOR</code>, plus <code>PETSC_DIR</code>, <code>SLEPC_DIR</code>, and <code>MPI_HOME</code> when those packages are installed. For PETSc, <code>PETSC_DIR</code> points to the installed SCLS prefix and <code>PETSC_ARCH</code> is intentionally empty: SCLS installs PETSc as a prefix-installed library, not an in-place source tree. <code>pkg-config</code>, CMake's <code>find_package</code>, and package-specific configuration scripts therefore work without further setup.</p>

    <p><code>LD_LIBRARY_PATH</code> is deliberately not modified. Every shared library in the stack has an rpath to its install prefix, so binaries find their dependencies at runtime without changing the loader search path for every child process. The activation script does remove stale SCLS entries from a previously active flavor, so switching stacks is a clean reactivation rather than an accumulating set of path entries.</p>

</section>

